WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.036
- Since 1967, CEA-Leti,

00:00:02.036 --> 00:00:04.530
here in Grenoble,
in the south-east of France,

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.540
has been one of the world's
largest research hubs

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.640
for electronics and nanotechnology.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.070
And by the mid-2000s, the
folks here had a problem.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.690
They had two clean rooms,

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.420
two carefully controlled
spaces with purified air

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.980
where research and testing could be done

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.770
without any dust or
contaminants getting in the way.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:24.060
But those two clean rooms
were 250 meters apart

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.560
on opposite sides of a road,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.180
which is the kind of thing that happens

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.120
when your campus steadily
grows over decades.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.080
So every time some tiny
component or piece of equipment

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.940
needed to be moved from one to the other,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.040
it had to be put in sterile packaging.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.350
Then, the researchers would
have to leave the clean room,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.450
take off all their gowns, and
gloves, and shoe covers,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.940
walk over, put on a whole
new set of protective gear,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:45.840
clean off the packaging,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.330
and then finally go in and unpack it.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.710
And you can see the solution.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.110
They built the world's
cleanest people mover,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.400
a clean room on wheels

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.250
that can somehow dock and
undock to the main buildings

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.710
without letting any outside air in.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.090
And we're going for a ride.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.540
- The clean room is about 11,000m².

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.810
Today at Leti, we have
about 2,000 employees,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.320
and 800 of them are
focusing on fabrication.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.310
We are making devices,
from components to sensors,

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.340
and we are mixing a wide
variety of technologies,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.220
including lasers, quantum technologies.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.050
We are able to print circuits

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.660
down to the 10-nanometer nodes.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.480
This is a device that you
will find in your phone

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.120
or in drones and specific systems.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.740
To speak about the dimension,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.460
we said that the thickness of
one hair is about 50 micron.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.000
So when we discuss a transistor
with a dimension of 10 nanometers,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.900
it's about 5,000 times
smaller than the hair.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.040
And the risk is that when one hair
is dropping on the surface,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.990
that, of course, the
circuit won't operate.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.510
- All of the horrible human stuff

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.423
is sealed inside many layers.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.690
Let's go ride a people mover.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.780
- We have to operate in a
very clean environment

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.170
since the source of contamination is us.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.890
So basically, we have
to wear special clothes

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.922
to protect the wafers, and
the dies, and the circuits,

00:02:10.922 --> 00:02:13.290
and the nanotransistors from us.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.470
- Obvious question, why
not just build a corridor?

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.910
Well, they did think about it,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.000
but that's a colossal amount of space

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.400
that would have to be
filled with ventilation

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.220
and air treatment equipment
all the way along,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.090
all constantly running and
maintained at huge expense.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.070
Also, they were worried
about how people would feel

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.070
walking down an isolated,
empty, narrow,

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:39.780
sealed-in 250-meter corridor
with no doors

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.190
where you can't see the other end

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.830
because it needs steps in the
middle to get over the road.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.703
Not great.

00:02:48.084 --> 00:02:51.417
[sliding door clicking]

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.383
Oh, there's the buttons!

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.450
Someone should probably join me in here!

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.800
- How clean is the clean room?

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.880
I would say compared to the hospital
and the surgical theater,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.030
the surgical theater
has probably 100 times

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.680
more particles in the air.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.330
And I would say that the house

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.380
has probably 1 million times
more particles in the air.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.490
We can see these bigger gray pipes,
and this is the way

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.250
that we are operating the
recirculation of the air

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.670
from the top to the basement
through the clean rooms.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:27.660
- I should point out,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.230
there's three or four people
crowded behind the camera here.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.533
I'm not being allowed in the
clean room funicular on my own.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:36.983
Here we go.

00:03:39.294 --> 00:03:41.640
And this is a clean room.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.050
The air is still being circulated

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.480
just as it was in each of the buildings.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.820
The concrete supports
that we're traveling over,

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.410
and all the powered systems

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.960
that are hauling the cables
that are moving us are,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.400
well, they're not off the shelf,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.290
but they are about as
standard as you can get

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.220
for something like this.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.110
They're manufactured by
a company called Poma

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.760
who make all sorts of ropeways.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.460
For this clean room that sits on the top,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.250
well, making this work was
up to the folks from the CEA.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.200
- So in the clean room,
we have a special team,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.110
which take care of the
cleaning of the surfaces

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.480
and make sure that no particles are stuck.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.070
We have to clean the shuttle as well.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.460
So the cleaning team is also
operating within the shuttle.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.650
- The problem is that this has to dock

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.900
and undock from the clean rooms.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.380
How does dust and outside
air not get inside?

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.900
The cabin slows down a
lot at the end of its run,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.430
and that's because it
has to position itself

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.430
extremely carefully, millimeter-accurate,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.710
to make sure the rubber seal on this door

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.240
matches up with the rubber seal

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.950
on the door of the clean room.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.090
They get connected.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.450
And then high-pressure air
blasts out anything in between.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.530
All the outside atmosphere is
exhausted by fresh, clean air.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.350
And this whole system, the
people mover and the clean room,

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.260
is kept at slightly higher
pressure than the outside.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.590
So if there ever is a leak, it's
only going to leak outwards.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.930
- When you enter the shuttle,
the door will close

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.390
and then you have to
wait for maybe 30 seconds

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.270
before there is a higher pressure inside.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.030
And then at the end, we
have the same operation.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.450
It's to depressurize the shuttle
to allow the door to open

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.780
and the operator to move in
the next clean room facility.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.510
- So why not just let
people keep walking over

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:16.410
the old-fashioned way?

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.230
Well, before this was installed,
the researchers here estimated

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.450
that transporting stuff
between the two rooms

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:24.270
was so inefficient that it
lost the time equivalent

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.610
of seven or eight full-time jobs.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.460
And rebuilding the clean rooms
would cost far, far more.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.980
This might seem a bit
unnecessary at first,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.240
but out of everything,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.290
it's probably the least expensive option.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.830
And I am not allowed through this door.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.360
I can't do the walk off into the distance!

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.903
I'm not allowed through this,
my badge doesn't let me.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:47.760
We go back!

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:48.633
Let's go back.

